Adhesion–Cohesion Astrophysical Simulation Engine (ACASE‑v9.8)

C. Matthew Blackwell — Independent Designer

AbstractThe Adhesion–Cohesion Astrophysical Simulation Engine (ACASE‑v9.8) introduces a novel computational framework for cosmic structure formation by integrating non‑standard adhesion and cohesion force laws with encrypted manifold evolution and GPU‑parallel astrophysical computation. Unlike conventional ΛCDM‑based simulations relying solely on gravitational N‑body dynamics and hydrodynamic pressure terms, ACASE‑v9.8 incorporates surface‑tension‑like adhesive interactions and tensor‑driven cohesive reinforcement, enabling the emergence of material‑like cosmic behaviors. Using observational datasets from Gaia DR3, JWST, and SDSS, the engine generates early, smooth, and highly stable filamentary structures; membrane‑like baryonic sheets; and sharply bounded dark matter halos exhibiting enhanced baryonic locking. A dynamic cryptographic hash,H512(Tr(I) ∥ det(I) ∥ I_total ∥ xᵢ),secures each manifold state, producing a tamper‑proof, self‑verifying simulation environment. Preliminary results suggest the existence of previously unmodeled bonding regimes in large‑scale cosmic evolution and demonstrate the feasibility of encrypted physics engines as a new class of scientific architecture. ACASE‑v9.8 represents a conceptual expansion of cosmological modeling, proposing adhesion–cohesion interactions as potential contributors to cosmic web stability and offering a unified physics–computation paradigm for next‑generation astrophysical simulation.

